ues running other tests; it reports all failures. The output shown only mentions test_encrypt_mixed_characters failing. But wait, the bijective test asserts shifted == 'exxego ex heas, 04:00!' which is wrong ('heas' vs 'hear'). Did it fail too? The error output only shows one failure... Maybe pytest output was truncated. Let me recheck: 'attack at dawn, 04:00!'\n\na\u2192e, t\u2192x, t\u2192x, a\u2192e, c\u2192g, k\u2192o \u2192 'exxego', ' ', a\u2192e, t\u2192x \u2192 'ex', ' ', d\u2192h, a\u2192e, w\u2192a, n\u2192r \u2192 'hear', ', 04:00!'. So shifted = 'exxego ex hear, 04:00!'. My assertion 'exxego ex heas, 04:00!' is wrong. Hmm, but the error output only shows the mixed test failing. Perhaps pytest reports failures in order and the output shown is truncated. Either way, I should fix both.\n\nWait, maybe the bijective test also failed but the user only pasted part. Regardless, I should correct both tests. Let me double-check 'heas': d\u2192h, a\u2192e, w\u2192a? Hmm wait \u2014 in my original test I wrote 'heas'. Let me verify: 'dawn' letters: d (index 3) \u2192 7 \u2192 'h'; a (0) \u2192 4 \u2192 'e'; w (22) \u2192 26 % 26 = 0 \u2192 'a'; n (13) \u2192 17 \u2192 'r'. So 'hear'. Yes, 'heas' was a typo.\n\nAlso recovered computation: shifted 'exxego ex hear, 04:00!' shifted back by 4: e\u2192a, x\u2192t, x\u2192t, e\u2192a, g\u2192c, o\u2192k \u2192 'attack'. Good.\n\nSo the corrected test script: fix 'Hipps, Wsvph!' and 'exxego ex hear, 04:00!'.\n\nLet me verify all other assertions once more:\n\n- encrypt('') == '' \u2713\n- encrypt('a') == 'e' \u2713\n- encrypt('v') == 'z': v is index 21, +4 = 25 \u2192 'z' \u2713\n- encrypt('z') == 'd' \u2713 (25+4=29%26=3)\n- encrypt('wxyz') == 'abcd': w(22)\u219226%26=0\u2192a, x(23)\u219227%26=1\u2192b, y(24)\u21922\u2192c, z(25)\u21923\u2192d \u2713\n- encrypt('hello') == 'lipps': h(7)\u219211\u2192l, e\u2192i, l\u2192p, l\u2192p, o\u2192s \u2713\n- encrypt('ABC') == 'ABC' \u2713 (uppercase not in lowercase d)\n- encrypt('0123') == '0123' \u2713\n- encrypt('!@#') == '!@#' \u2713\n- encrypt(' ') == ' ' \u2713\n- encrypt('Hello, World!') == 'Hipps, Wsvph!' \u2713 (fixed)\n- encrypt('abc XYZ 123') == 'efg XYZ 123' \u2713\n\nThe bijective test: use the correct shifted value. Also note the walrus in generator expression: `d[(d.index(c) - 4) % 26] if c in (d := 'abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz') else c` \u2014 evaluation order: the condition `c in (d := ...)` is evaluated first (condition is evaluated before the value expression in a conditional expression), so d is defined before use. That works, but to keep it simple and avoid any confusion, I'll define d at the top of the function instead. That's cleaner.\n\nFinal script:\n\n```python\nimport pytest\n\nfrom f089 import encrypt\n\n\ndef test_encrypt_empty_string():\n    assert encrypt('') == ''\n\n\ndef test_encrypt_lowercase_letters():\n    assert encrypt('a') == 'e'\n    assert encrypt('v') == 'z'\n    assert encrypt('z') == 'd'\n    assert encrypt('wxyz') == 'abcd'\n    assert encrypt('hello') == 'lipps'\n\n\ndef test_encrypt_non_lowercase_characters():\n    assert encrypt('ABC') == 'ABC'\n    assert encrypt('0123') == '0123'\n    assert encrypt('!@#') == '!@#'\n    assert encrypt(' ') == ' '\n\n\ndef test_encrypt_mixed_characters():\n    # Exercises both branches of the if/else in a single call.\n    # Only lowercase letters are shifted; uppercase, punctuation and\n    # digits pass through unchanged.\n    assert encrypt('Hello, World!') == 'Hipps, Wsvph!'\n    assert encrypt('abc XYZ 123') == 'efg XYZ 123'\n\n\ndef test_encrypt_is_bijective_with_shift_four():\n    # Postcondition: applying the inverse shift recovers the original text.\n    d = 'abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz'\n    original = 'attack at dawn, 04:00!'\n    shifted = encrypt(original)\n    assert shifted == 'exxego ex hear, 04:00!'\n    recovered = ''.join(\n        d[(d.index(c) - 4) % 26] if c in d else c\n        for c in shifted\n    )\n    assert recovered == original\n`